  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given a matrix CCC that has NNN rows and MMM columns where each entry is 111 or 000 or x\\texttt{x}x. Here x\\texttt{x}x denotes a `don't care` value.\n\nYou can perform the following operation on the matrix:\n\n\nPick any column jjj such that 1≤j≤M1 \\le j \\le M1≤j≤M.\nAssign Ci,j→Ci,j⊕1C_{i, j} \\to C_{i, j} \\oplus 1Ci,j​→Ci,j​⊕1 for all 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N\nYou are given that:\n\n\n0⊕1=10 \\oplus 1 = 10⊕1=1\n1⊕1=01 \\oplus 1 = 01⊕1=0\nx⊕1=x\\texttt{x} \\oplus 1 = \\texttt{x}x⊕1=x\nwhere ⊕\\oplus⊕ denotes the bitwise XOR operation.\nYou have to perform any number of operations (possibly 000) such that each row in the matrix has **at most** a single 111.\n\nDetermine if it's possible to achieve this. Print `YES` if it's possible, else print `NO`.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line will contain TTT - the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nThe first line of each test case has two integers NNN and MMM - the number of rows and number of columns respectively.\nThe next MMM lines of the test case contain NNN characters each where the jjj-th character in the iii-th line denotes Ci,jC_{i, j}Ci,j​.\n\n\nOutput Format\nPrint `YES` if it's possible to perform any number of operations so that each row has at most a single 111, else print `NO`.\n\n\nYou may print each character of `Yes` and `No` in uppercase or lowercase (for example, `yes`, `yEs`, `YES` will be considered identical).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤4001 \\le T \\le 4001≤T≤400",
      "1≤N,M≤10001 \\le N, M \\le 10001≤N,M≤1000",
      "Sum of N⋅MN \\cdot MN⋅M over all test cases does not exceed 10610^6106"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2 2\n00\n11\n4 5\n10x1x\n1xx11\nxx101\n1x11x\n3 3\n101\n010\n100",
        "output": "YES\nYES\nNO",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case-1: We just perform one operation on the first column.\nTest case-2: We perform the operations on the columns 1,31, 31,3 and 555.\nTest case-3: It can be proven that it is impossible to satisfy the given conditions using any number of operations."
      }
    ]
